  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively provide several relevant ingredients: rapid cooling can kinetically retain metastable phases (R10, complemented by the slow-cooling result in R11); melt processing and composition changes can produce Laves-containing or other dual-phase HEAs (R13, R14, R16, R17, R19, R20); and an antiferromagnetic constituent can reduce the saturation magnetization of a Fe/Co-based HEA (R5). Other records establish that phase coexistence and hard secondary phases can alter defect motion or properties. However, none discloses rapid quenching of FeCoNiTi to obtain the specific fcc–C14 constitution, the compositionally matched C14-dominant control, Fe/Co/Ni–Ti antiferromagnetic sublattice coupling, assignment of the multiple magnetic transitions, or C14-assisted orbital/spin-canted domain-wall pinning producing high-temperature coercivity. Thus the literature points generally toward processing-controlled dual phases and magnetic-moment suppression, but reconstructing the specific intervention and magnetic mechanism would require a substantial new idea."
